# Git attributes for line endings and file type handling

# Set line endings to LF for all files
* text eol=lf

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# BINARY ARCHIVES -- never subject these to the text filter above.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Why this rule exists
# --------------------
# The catch-all `* text eol=lf` above applies Git's *text* filter to every file
# it does not explicitly exclude, and the text filter's job is to rewrite line
# endings.  It does that by deleting the CR byte of every CRLF pair on the way
# into the object store.
#
# That is harmless for source code and catastrophic for a Parquet file, because
# a Parquet file is a sequence of length-prefixed compressed pages with absolute
# byte offsets recorded in its footer.  Deleting a byte shifts every offset
# after it, and the footer no longer describes the file -- pyarrow then reports
# "Corrupt snappy compressed data" or "Couldn't deserialize thrift".
#
# This is not hypothetical.  It happened here: with no `*.parquet` rule, 488 of
# the 13,637 tracked archives were committed with their CRLF pairs collapsed,
# and a `git checkout` writes those corrupted bytes back over the working tree.
# Measured on a single file:
#
#     worktree   194,840 bytes, 2 CRLF pairs  -> reads fine, 4,418 rows
#     committed  194,838 bytes, 0 CRLF pairs  -> "Corrupt snappy compressed data"
#
# The two bytes are the whole difference.  `*.parquet binary` is therefore not a
# tidy-up; it is the fix.
#
# `binary` is used rather than `-text` because `binary` is a macro that expands
# to `-diff -merge -text`, which is what a file that must never be touched by a
# content filter wants.
*.parquet binary

# Other binary payloads the archive and the tools write.  Same reasoning.
*.feather binary
*.arrow binary
*.arrow5 binary
*.orc binary
*.avro binary
*.pb binary
*.h5 binary
*.hdf5 binary
*.npy binary
*.npz binary
*.pkl binary
*.pickle binary
*.joblib binary
*.db binary
*.sqlite binary
*.sqlite3 binary

# CSV stays text.  This line is a no-op relative to the catch-all above -- it is
# spelled out because the archive's CSVs are read with `engine="pyarrow"` for
# speed and are compared byte-for-byte by the store, so it is worth being
# explicit that they are *not* binary and never were intended to be.
*.csv text eol=lf

# Set binary files as binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.svg binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.tar binary
*.gz binary

# Set executable files as executable
*.sh executable
*.py executable
*.js executable
*.sh executable

